export const INTRODUCTION: IntroBlock[] = [
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "This book exists because ADI believes there is another way to understand and respond to human experience. It is a way that helps us see more clearly, learn more wisely, recover choice, recognise what is true about ourselves, and move with greater freedom and purpose. It is also a way that helps people, groups and organisations function more fully from their capability, purpose and potential." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "We call this **the Third Way**." },
  { kind: "heading", text: "Why a Third Way?" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "Much of the way we are taught to understand people and situations is binary. Something is good or bad, right or wrong, successful or failing. A person may be seen as compliant or difficult, worthy or unworthy, capable or incapable. An organisation may be described as succeeding or failing. A community may be spoken about through what is wrong with it or through an idealised version of what people would like it to be." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "These distinctions can sometimes be useful. The difficulty comes when one part of the picture becomes the whole picture." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "The Third Way asks us to look beyond the binary without abandoning truth, responsibility or standards. It asks us to understand more fully, to hold complexity, to learn from what has happened, and to decide what creates the greatest possibility for movement. It is not the middle point between two extremes. **It is a different quality of seeing.**" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "That difference matters. We can say that behaviour caused harm, that a decision was wrong, that a standard was not met, that something must stop or that responsibility is required. The Third Way does not ask us to pretend otherwise. What it asks us to resist is turning a behaviour, an experience, a failure, an injury or somebody else’s response to us into the complete definition of a person." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "There is always more to see." },
  { kind: "heading", text: "Experience as information" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "Human beings are constantly learning from experience, but we do not always learn things that make us freer. We can learn from rejection never to put ourselves forward again, from betrayal never to trust, from criticism to make ourselves smaller, or from being overlooked to believe that perhaps we are not ready. We can learn from environments in which we have had to prove ourselves that achievement is the only place where we are safe. We can learn to carry everyone because needing other people once felt dangerous." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "The fact that something has been learned does not automatically make it true or useful for the life we are trying to live now. The Third Way therefore asks more of experience. What happened? What did I make it mean? What did I learn to do because of it? What did that response protect? What belongs to me in this experience, and what belongs to others? And what is true now?" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "This is where the deeper work begins." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "Sometimes what helped us survive becomes so familiar that we stop seeing it as something we learned to do. We begin to experience it simply as who we are. We may become the person who never asks for help, the person who has to stay in control, the person who keeps quiet even when they have something important to say, the person who achieves and still never feels enough, the person who carries everyone, or the person who expects to be misunderstood." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "These ways of being may have helped us through something, and they deserve to be understood before they are discarded. ADI asks us to look again. What happened that made this way of being necessary? What did it feel like? What did we have to tell ourselves so that we could keep going? What did we come to believe about ourselves because of it?" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "Then comes a question that runs through much of our work:" },
  { kind: "callout", text: "Is that really the whole truth about you?" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "That question is not an instruction to think positively. It is an invitation to look at the evidence. Where have you already shown courage? What have you navigated, created, led, learned or made possible? What qualities did that require? One experience, one workplace, one relationship, one period of difficulty or one person’s response to us should not become the sole authority on who we are." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "Sometimes the evidence begins to return us to ourselves. We recognise capabilities that have become difficult to see, hear our own voice differently, recover trust in what we know, and begin to distinguish between what happened to us, what we had to become in response, and what remains true about us." },
  { kind: "callout", text: "Congruence is what happens when identity no longer has to change clothes to enter different rooms." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "The person does not have to manufacture a new self. They can begin to live from a fuller one." },
  { kind: "heading", text: "The crossing" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "Recognition can bring relief, but recognition is a beginning. There is often a period when the old way of being no longer fits and the new way is not yet secure. A person may know that a job, relationship, role, belief or way of protecting themselves can no longer hold their life, while still feeling frightened of what will replace it." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "The familiar can feel safer simply because it is familiar. We can even find ourselves drawn towards the same underlying pattern in a different form: another relationship, another workplace, another role or another situation that looks new but eventually reproduces something we have already lived. This is why learning from experience matters. Movement requires more than changing the setting. It asks us to recognise what is repeating and to become able to choose differently." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "This is also where relationship and community matter. Sometimes another person has to hold sight of a possibility while we are still learning to hold it for ourselves. Sometimes we need a space in which we can say, “I know this no longer fits me, but I do not yet know what comes next.”" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "ADI seeks to create those spaces: spaces in which difficult experiences can be examined closely enough for their meaning and learning to become visible; spaces in which people can be seen, believed and challenged without being made smaller; spaces in which questions can be asked without the question itself becoming a sentence; and spaces in which we can begin to separate what we have carried from what we are called to become." },
  { kind: "heading", text: "Learning that becomes useful" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "The Third Way does not ask us to make painful experiences good. Harm is harm, loss is loss, and injustice is injustice. But once something has happened, another question becomes possible: **what do I now know because I have lived through this?**" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "What have I learned about myself, people, power, relationships, leadership, systems, fear, survival or possibility? What part of what happened belongs to me to understand, change or take responsibility for? What belongs to somebody else and should not become mine to carry? What can I take from the experience that enlarges rather than restricts my choices? What can that knowledge now make possible? How might it help someone else? What does it reveal about what I am here to contribute?" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "Experience can become learning. Learning can become usable knowledge. Usable knowledge can become contribution. Contribution can reconnect us with purpose." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "The work is not simply to feel better about what happened. **The work is to become able to live at the scale of one’s gift.**" },
  { kind: "heading", text: "Why ADI begins with Black people" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "ADI begins with Black people because our history and contemporary experience provide a particularly concentrated place from which to examine what happens when human beings are repeatedly defined, constrained, interpreted, measured and treated through systems that do not begin with the fullness of who they are." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "Those experiences matter and must be named. They are also not the whole story." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "Black people have continued to think, create, discover, build, teach, lead, invent, love, organise, care, produce knowledge and shape the world. We did, and we do. We created, and we create. We built, and we build. We led, and we lead. We imagined tomorrow, and we continue to imagine it." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "The evidence is historical, contemporary, collective, personal and global. We look at that evidence because people need more than reassurance. They need truth." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "Not as triumphalism. As truth." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "The same is true at the level of the individual. When somebody has spent years being treated as though they are too much, not enough, difficult, incapable, threatening, invisible or out of place, ADI does not simply tell them that they are wonderful. We look together at the evidence. What have you done? What have you survived? What have you built? What do people seek you out for? What keeps appearing in your life? What capacity have you been treating as ordinary because it comes naturally to you? What becomes possible if you stop organising your life around somebody else’s smaller interpretation of you?" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "This is where freedom begins to become practical." },
  { kind: "heading", text: "From freedom to impact" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "Freedom creates room for self-authority. Self-authority makes it possible to recognise gift. Gift can find direction in purpose. Purpose can become achievement. Achievement can influence other people, organisations and systems. Influence can become contribution, contribution can become building, building can create local, national and global impact, and impact can become legacy." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "Achievement is what a person brings into being. **Impact is what that achievement changes beyond them.**" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "ADI is interested in both." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "We are interested in what becomes possible when Black people are able to live and lead at the full scale of their gift, purpose and capability. We are interested in people who build businesses, transform organisations, create knowledge, develop communities, influence professions, produce art, lead families, improve public services, create wealth, mentor others and shape the world around them." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "We are equally interested in the internal freedom that allows those things to be done without the person having continually to abandon themselves in the process." },
  { kind: "heading", text: "An invitation" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "The Third Way is therefore both a philosophy and a practice. It offers a way of understanding human experience and a way of working with what that understanding reveals." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "It is relevant to individual growth and mindset, relationships, coaching, leadership and development, facilitation, organisations, communities, and wider national and social contexts. The settings may be different, but many of the questions remain relevant: what is happening, what has shaped it, what has been learned, what belongs to whom, what is true now, what needs to change, and what can support wiser movement?" },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "The philosophy provides the way of seeing. Its practice is how that way of seeing is brought into thought, relationship, conversation, choice and action." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "The Third Way asks us to pause before the first explanation becomes the only explanation, to ask questions, to listen for what lies beneath presentation, to notice what experience has taught us, to recognise what we have internalised, to become curious about what our ways of coping have protected, and to test what we believe against the evidence." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "It asks us to distinguish what belongs to us from what belongs to others, to recognise when old patterns are presenting themselves in new forms, to retrieve what has become difficult to see, to tolerate the uncertainty of living differently, allow possibility to return, and then move." },
  { kind: "paragraph", text: "Pause. Look again. Ask what is being shown. Learn from experience. Recover choice. Recover self-authority. Recognise the gift. Live at the scale of it. Move with purpose. Achieve. Influence. Build. Contribute. Create global impact. Leave a legacy." },
  { kind: "callout", text: "And do not make yourself, or anyone else, smaller in the process." },
];
